<body>
<p align=3D"CENTER">
<b>BERKSHIRE HATHAWAY INC.</b>
</p>

<pre>

<b>To the Shareholders of Berkshire Hathaway Inc.:</b>

     Our gain in net worth during 1989 was $1.515 billion, or=20
44.4%. Over the last 25 years (that is, since present management=20
took over) our per-share book value has grown from $19.46 to=20
$4,296.01, or at a rate of 23.8% compounded annually.

     What counts, however, is intrinsic value - the figure=20
indicating what all of our constituent businesses are rationally=20
worth. With perfect foresight, this number can be calculated by=20
taking all future cash flows of a business - in and out - and=20
discounting them at prevailing interest rates. So valued, all=20
businesses, from manufacturers of buggy whips to operators of=20
cellular phones, become economic equals.=20

     Back when Berkshire's book value was $19.46, intrinsic=20
value was somewhat less because the book value was entirely tied=20
up in a textile business not worth the figure at which it was=20
carried. Now most of our businesses are worth far more than their=20
carrying values. This agreeable evolution from a discount to a=20
premium means that Berkshire's intrinsic business value has=20
compounded at a rate that somewhat exceeds our 23.8% annual=20
growth in book value.

     The rear-view mirror is one thing; the windshield is=20
another. A large portion of our book value is represented by=20
equity securities that, with minor exceptions, are carried on our=20
balance sheet at current market values. At yearend these=20
securities were valued at higher prices, relative to their own=20
intrinsic business values, than has been the case in the past.=20
One reason is the buoyant 1989 stock market. More important, the=20
virtues of these businesses have been widely recognized. Whereas=20
once their stock prices were inappropriately low, they are not=20
now.

     We will keep most of our major holdings, regardless of how=20
they are priced relative to intrinsic business value. This 'til-
death-do-us-part attitude, combined with the full prices these=20
holdings command, means that they cannot be expected to push up=20
Berkshire's value in the future as sharply as in the past. In=20
other words, our performance to date has benefited from a double-
dip: (1) the exceptional gains in intrinsic value that our=20
portfolio companies have achieved; (2) the additional bonus we=20
realized as the market appropriately "corrected" the prices of=20
these companies, raising their valuations in relation to those of=20
the average business. We will continue to benefit from good gains=20
in business value that we feel confident our portfolio companies=20
will make. But our "catch-up" rewards have been realized, which=20
means we'll have to settle for a single-dip in the future.

     We face another obstacle: In a finite world, high growth=20
rates must self-destruct. If the base from which the growth is=20
taking place is tiny, this law may not operate for a time. But=20
when the base balloons, the party ends: A high growth rate=20
eventually forges its own anchor.

     Carl Sagan has entertainingly described this phenomenon,=20
musing about the destiny of bacteria that reproduce by dividing=20
into two every 15 minutes. Says Sagan: "That means four doublings=20
an hour, and 96 doublings a day. Although a bacterium weighs only=20
about a trillionth of a gram, its descendants, after a day of=20
wild asexual abandon, will collectively weigh as much as a=20
mountain...in two days, more than the sun - and before very long,=20
everything in the universe will be made of bacteria." Not to=20
worry, says Sagan:  Some obstacle always impedes this kind of=20
exponential growth. "The bugs run out of food, or they poison=20
each other, or they are shy about reproducing in public." =20

     Even on bad days, Charlie Munger (Berkshire's Vice Chairman=20
and my partner) and I do not think of Berkshire as a bacterium.=20
Nor, to our unending sorrow, have we found a way to double its=20
net worth every 15 minutes. Furthermore, we are not the least bit=20
shy about reproducing - financially - in public. Nevertheless,=20
Sagan's observations apply. From Berkshire's present base of $4.9=20
billion in net worth, we will find it much more difficult to=20
average 15% annual growth in book value than we did to average=20
23.8% from the $22 million we began with.

<b>Taxes</b>

     Our 1989 gain of $1.5 billion was achieved after we took a=20
charge of about $712 million for income taxes. In addition,=20
Berkshire's share of the income taxes paid by its five major=20
investees totaled about $175 million.=20

     Of this year's tax charge, about $172 million will be paid=20
currently; the remainder, $540 million, is deferred. Almost all=20
of the deferred portion relates to the 1989 increase in=20
unrealized profits in our common stock holdings. Against this=20
increase, we have reserved a 34% tax.

     We also carry reserves at that rate against all unrealized=20
profits generated in 1987 and 1988. But, as we explained last=20
year, the unrealized gains we amassed before 1987 - about $1.2=20
billion - carry reserves booked at the 28% tax rate that then=20
prevailed.=20

     A new accounting rule is likely to be adopted that will=20
require companies to reserve against all gains at the current tax=20
rate, whatever it may be. With the rate at 34%, such a rule would=20
increase our deferred tax liability, and decrease our net worth,=20
by about $71 million - the result of raising the reserve on our=20
pre-1987 gain by six percentage points. Because the proposed rule=20
has sparked widespread controversy and its final form is unclear,=20
we have not yet made this change.

     As you can see from our balance sheet on page 27, we would=20
owe taxes of more than $1.1 billion were we to sell all of our=20
securities at year-end market values. Is this $1.1 billion=20
liability equal, or even similar, to a $1.1 billion liability=20
payable to a trade creditor 15 days after the end of the year? =20
Obviously not - despite the fact that both items have exactly the=20
same effect on audited net worth, reducing it by $1.1 billion.

     On the other hand, is this liability for deferred taxes a=20
meaningless accounting fiction because its payment can be=20
triggered only by the sale of stocks that, in very large part, we=20
have no intention of selling?  Again, the answer is no.=20

     In economic terms, the liability resembles an interest-free=20
loan from the U.S. Treasury that comes due only at our election=20
(unless, of course, Congress moves to tax gains before they are=20
realized). This "loan" is peculiar in other respects as well: It=20
can be used only to finance the ownership of the particular,=20
appreciated stocks and it fluctuates in size - daily as market=20
prices change and periodically if tax rates change. In effect,=20
this deferred tax liability is equivalent to a very large=20
transfer tax that is payable only if we elect to move from one=20
asset to another. Indeed, we sold some relatively small holdings=20
in 1989, incurring about $76 million of "transfer" tax on $224=20
million of gains.

     Because of the way the tax law works, the Rip Van Winkle=20
style of investing that we favor - if successful - has an=20
important mathematical edge over a more frenzied approach. Let's=20
look at an extreme comparison.

     Imagine that Berkshire had only $1, which we put in a=20
security that doubled by yearend and was then sold. Imagine=20
further that we used the after-tax proceeds to repeat this=20
process in each of the next 19 years, scoring a double each time.=20
At the end of the 20 years, the 34% capital gains tax that we=20
would have paid on the profits from each sale would have=20
delivered about $13,000 to the government and we would be left=20
with about $25,250. Not bad. If, however, we made a single=20
fantastic investment that <i>itself</i> doubled 20 times during the 20=20
years, our dollar would grow to $1,048,576. Were we then to cash=20
out, we would pay a 34% tax of roughly $356,500 and be left with=20
about $692,000.=20

     The sole reason for this staggering difference in results=20
would be the timing of tax payments. Interestingly, the=20
government would gain from Scenario 2 in exactly the same 27:1=20
ratio as we - taking in taxes of $356,500 vs. $13,000 - though,=20
admittedly, it would have to wait for its money.

     We have not, we should stress, adopted our strategy=20
favoring long-term investment commitments because of these=20
mathematics. Indeed, it is possible we could earn greater after-
tax returns by moving rather frequently from one investment to=20
another. Many years ago, that's exactly what Charlie and I did.

     Now we would rather stay put, even if that means slightly=20
lower returns. Our reason is simple: We have found splendid=20
business relationships to be so rare and so enjoyable that we=20
want to retain all we develop.  This decision is particularly=20
easy for us because we feel that these relationships will produce
good - though perhaps not optimal - financial results.=20
Considering that, we think it makes little sense for us to give=20
up time with people we know to be interesting and admirable for=20
time with others we do not know and who are likely to have human=20
qualities far closer to average. That would be akin to marrying=20
for money - a mistake under most circumstances, insanity if one=20
is already rich.


