     We refer you also to pages 45-51, where we have rearranged=20
Berkshire's financial data into four segments. These correspond=20
to the way Charlie and I think about the business and should help=20
you calculate Berkshire's intrinsic value. Shown on these pages=20
are balance sheets and earnings statements for:  (1) our=20
insurance operations, with their major investment positions=20
itemized; (2) our manufacturing, publishing and retailing=20
businesses, leaving aside certain non-operating assets and=20
purchase-price accounting adjustments; (3) our subsidiaries=20
engaged in finance-type operations, which are Mutual Savings and=20
Scott Fetzer Financial; and (4) an all-other category that=20
includes the non-operating assets (primarily marketable=20
securities) held by the companies in segment (2), all purchase=20
price accounting adjustments, and various assets and debts of the=20
Wesco and Berkshire parent companies.

     If you combine the earnings and net worths of these four=20
segments, you will derive totals matching those shown on our GAAP=20
statements. However, I want to emphasize that this four-category=20
presentation does not fall within the purview of our auditors,=20
who in no way bless it.

     In addition to our reported earnings, we also benefit from=20
significant earnings of investees that standard accounting rules=20
do not permit us to report. On page 15, we list five major=20
investees from which we received dividends in 1989 of about $45=20
million, after taxes. However, our share of the <i>retained</i> earnings=20
of these investees totaled about $212 million last year, not=20
counting large capital gains realized by GEICO and Coca-Cola. If=20
this $212 million had been distributed to us, our own operating=20
earnings, after the payment of additional taxes, would have been=20
close to $500 million rather than the $300 million shown in the=20
table.

     The question you must decide is whether these undistributed=20
earnings are as valuable to us as those we report. We believe=20
they are - and even think they may be more valuable. The reason=20
for this a-bird-in-the-bush-may-be-worth-two-in-the-hand =20
conclusion is that earnings retained  by these  investees will =20
be deployed  by talented,  owner-oriented  managers  who=20
sometimes have better uses for these funds in their own=20
businesses than we would have in ours. I would not make such a=20
generous assessment of most managements, but it is appropriate in=20
these cases.

     In our view, Berkshire's fundamental earning power is best=20
measured by a "look-through" approach, in which we append our=20
share of the operating earnings retained by our investees to our=20
own reported operating earnings, excluding capital gains in both=20
instances. For our intrinsic business value to grow at an average=20
of 15% per year, our "look-through" earnings must grow at about=20
the same pace. We'll need plenty of help from our present=20
investees, and also need to add a new one from time to time, in=20
order to reach this 15% goal.


<b>Non-Insurance Operations</b>

     In the past, we have labeled our major manufacturing,=20
publishing and retail operations "The Sainted Seven." With our=20
acquisition of Borsheim's early in 1989, the challenge was to=20
find a new title both alliterative and appropriate. We failed:=20
Let's call the group "The Sainted Seven Plus One."

     This divine assemblage - Borsheim's, The Buffalo News,=20
Fechheimer Bros., Kirby, Nebraska Furniture Mart, Scott Fetzer=20
Manufacturing Group, See's Candies, World Book - is a collection=20
of businesses with economic characteristics that range from good=20
to superb. Its managers range from superb to superb.

     Most of these managers have no need to work for a living;=20
they show up at the ballpark because they like to hit home runs.=20
And that's exactly what they do. Their combined financial=20
statements (including those of some smaller operations), shown on=20
page 49, illustrate just how outstanding their performance is. On=20
an historical accounting basis, after-tax earnings of these=20
operations were 57% on average equity capital. Moreover, this=20
return was achieved with no net leverage: Cash equivalents have=20
matched funded debt. When I call off the names of our managers -=20
the Blumkin, Friedman and Heldman families, Chuck Huggins, Stan=20
Lipsey, and Ralph Schey - I feel the same glow that Miller=20
Huggins must have experienced when he announced the lineup of his=20
1927 New York Yankees.

     Let's take a look, business by business:

<b>o</b>     In its first year with Berkshire, Borsheim's met all=20
expectations. Sales rose significantly and are now considerably=20
better than twice what they were four years ago when the company=20
moved to its present location. In the six years prior to the=20
move, sales had also doubled. Ike Friedman, Borsheim's managing=20
genius - and I mean that - has only one speed: fast-forward.

     If you haven't been there, you've never seen a jewelry store=20
like Borsheim's. Because of the huge volume it does at one=20
location, the store can maintain an enormous selection across all=20
price ranges. For the same reason, it can hold its expense ratio=20
to about one-third that prevailing at jewelry stores offering=20
comparable merchandise. The store's tight control of expenses,=20
accompanied by its unusual buying power, enable it to offer=20
prices far lower than those of other jewelers. These prices, in=20
turn, generate even more volume, and so the circle goes 'round=20
and 'round. The end result is store traffic as high as 4,000=20
people on seasonally-busy days.

     Ike Friedman is not only a superb businessman and a great=20
showman but also a man of integrity. We bought the business=20
without an audit, and all of our surprises have been on the plus=20
side. "If you don't know jewelry, know your jeweler" makes sense=20
whether you are buying the whole business or a tiny diamond.

     A story will illustrate why I enjoy Ike so much: Every two=20
years I'm part of an informal group that gathers to have fun and=20
explore a few subjects. Last September, meeting at Bishop's Lodge=20
in Santa Fe, we asked Ike, his wife Roz, and his son Alan to come=20
by and educate us on jewels and the jewelry business.

     Ike decided to dazzle the group, so he brought from Omaha=20
about $20 million of particularly fancy merchandise. I was=20
somewhat apprehensive - Bishop's Lodge is no Fort Knox - and I=20
mentioned my concern to Ike at our opening party the evening=20
before his presentation. Ike took me aside. "See that safe?" he=20
said. "This afternoon we changed the combination and now even the=20
hotel management doesn't know what it is." I breathed easier. Ike=20
went on: "See those two big fellows with guns on their hips? =20
They'll be guarding the safe all night." I now was ready to=20
rejoin the party. But Ike leaned closer: "And besides, Warren,"=20
he confided, "the jewels aren't in the safe."

     How can we miss with a fellow like that - particularly when=20
he comes equipped with a talented and energetic family, Alan,=20
Marvin Cohn, and Don Yale.

<b>o</b>     At See's Candies we had an 8% increase in pounds sold, even=20
though 1988 was itself a record year. Included in the 1989=20
performance were excellent same-store poundage gains, our first=20
in many years.

     Advertising played an important role in this outstanding=20
performance. We increased total advertising expenditures from $4=20
million to $5 million and also got copy from our agency, Hal=20
Riney &amp; Partners, Inc., that was 100% on the money in conveying=20
the qualities that make See's special.

     In our media businesses, such as the Buffalo News, we sell=20
advertising. In other businesses, such as See's, we are buyers.=20
When we buy, we practice exactly what we preach when we sell. At=20
See's, we more than tripled our expenditures on newspaper=20
advertising last year, to the highest percentage of sales that I=20
can remember. The payoff was terrific, and we thank both Hal=20
Riney and the power of well-directed newspaper advertising for=20
this result.

     See's splendid performances have become routine. But there=20
is nothing routine about the management of Chuck Huggins: His=20
daily involvement with all aspects of production and sales=20
imparts a quality-and-service message to the thousands of=20
employees we need to produce and distribute over 27 million=20
pounds of candy annually. In a company with 225 shops and a=20
massive mail order and phone business, it is no small trick to=20
run things so that virtually every customer leaves happy. Chuck=20
makes it look easy.=20

<b>o</b>     The Nebraska Furniture Mart had record sales and excellent=20
earnings in 1989, but there was one sad note. Mrs. B - Rose=20
Blumkin, who started the company 52 years ago with $500 - quit in=20
May, after disagreeing with other members of the Blumkin=20
family/management about the remodeling and operation of the=20
carpet department.

     Mrs. B probably has made more smart business decisions than=20
any living American, but in this particular case I believe the=20
other members of the family were entirely correct: Over the past=20
three years, while the store's other departments increased sales=20
by 24%, carpet sales declined by 17% (but not because of any lack=20
of sales ability by Mrs. B, who has always personally sold far=20
more merchandise than any other salesperson in the store).

     You will be pleased to know that Mrs. B continues to make=20
Horatio Alger's heroes look like victims of tired blood. At age=20
96 she has started a new business selling - what else? - carpet=20
and furniture. And as always, she works seven days a week.

     At the Mart Louie, Ron, and Irv Blumkin continue to propel=20
what is by far the largest and most successful home furnishings=20
store in the country. They are outstanding merchants, outstanding=20
managers, and a joy to be associated with. One reading on their=20
acumen: In the fourth quarter of 1989, the carpet department=20
registered a 75.3% consumer share in the Omaha market, up from=20
67.7% a year earlier and over six times that of its nearest=20
competitor.

     NFM and Borsheim's follow precisely the same formula for=20
success: (1) unparalleled depth and breadth of merchandise at one=20
location; (2) the lowest operating costs in the business; (3) the=20
shrewdest of buying, made possible in part by the huge volumes=20
purchased; (4) gross margins, and therefore prices, far below=20
competitors'; and (5) friendly personalized service with family=20
members on hand at all times.

     Another plug for newspapers: NFM increased its linage in the=20
local paper by over 20% in 1989 - off a record 1988 - and remains=20
the paper's largest ROP advertiser by far. (ROP advertising is=20
the kind printed in the paper, as opposed to that in preprinted=20
inserts.) To my knowledge, Omaha is the only city in which a home=20
furnishings store is the advertising leader. Many retailers cut=20
space purchases in 1989; our experience at See's and NFM would=20
indicate they made a major mistake.

<b>o</b>     The Buffalo News continued to star in 1989 in three=20
important ways: First, among major metropolitan papers, both=20
daily and Sunday, the News is number one in household penetration=20
- the percentage of local households that purchase it each day.=20
Second, in "news hole" - the portion of the paper devoted to news=20
- the paper stood at 50.1% in 1989 vs. 49.5% in 1988, a level=20
again making it more news-rich than any comparable American=20
paper. Third, in a year that saw profits slip at many major=20
papers, the News set its seventh consecutive profit record.

     To some extent, these three factors are related, though=20
obviously a high-percentage news hole, by itself, reduces profits=20
significantly. A large and intelligently-utilized news hole,=20
however, attracts a wide spectrum of readers and thereby boosts=20
penetration. High penetration, in turn, makes a newspaper=20
particularly valuable to retailers since it allows them to talk=20
to the entire community through a single "megaphone." A low-
penetration paper is a far less compelling purchase for many=20
advertisers and will eventually suffer in both ad rates and=20
profits.

     It should be emphasized that our excellent penetration is=20
neither an accident nor automatic. The population of Erie County,=20
home territory of the News, has been falling - from 1,113,000 in=20
1970 to 1,015,000 in 1980 to an estimated 966,000 in 1988.=20
Circulation figures tell a different story. In 1975, shortly=20
before we started our Sunday edition, the Courier-Express, a=20
long-established Buffalo paper, was selling 207,500 Sunday copies=20
in Erie County. Last year - with population at least 5% lower -=20
the News sold an average of 292,700 copies. I believe that in no=20
other major Sunday market has there been anything close to that=20
increase in penetration.

     When this kind of gain is made - and when a paper attains an=20
unequaled degree of acceptance in its home town - someone is=20
doing something right. In this case major credit clearly belongs=20
to Murray Light, our long-time editor who daily creates an=20
informative, useful, and interesting product. Credit should go=20
also to the Circulation and Production Departments: A paper that=20
is frequently late, because of production problems or=20
distribution weaknesses, will lose customers, no matter how=20
strong its editorial content.

     Stan Lipsey, publisher of the News, has produced profits=20
fully up to the strength of our product. I believe Stan's=20
managerial skills deliver at least five extra percentage points=20
in profit margin compared to the earnings that would be achieved=20
by an average manager given the same circumstances. That is an=20
amazing performance, and one that could only be produced by a=20
talented manager who knows - and cares - about every nut and bolt=20
of the business.=20

     Stan's knowledge and talents, it should be emphasized,=20
extend to the editorial product. His early years in the business=20
were spent on the news side and he played a key role in=20
developing and editing a series of stories that in 1972 won a=20
Pulitzer Prize for the Sun Newspaper of Omaha. Stan and I have=20
worked together for over 20 years, through some bad times as well=20
as good, and I could not ask for a better partner.

<b>o</b>     At Fechheimer, the Heldman clan - Bob, George, Gary,=20
Roger and Fred - continue their extraordinary performance. Profits=20
in 1989 were down somewhat because of problems the business=20
experienced in integrating a major 1988 acquisition. These=20
problems will be ironed out in time. Meanwhile, return on invested=20
capital at Fechheimer remains splendid.

     Like all of our managers, the Heldmans have an exceptional=20
command of the details of their business. At last year's annual=20
meeting I mentioned that when a prisoner enters San Quentin, Bob=20
and George probably know his shirt size. That's only a slight=20
exaggeration: No matter what area of the country is being=20
