     The combined ratio represents total insurance costs (losses=20
incurred plus expenses) compared to revenue from premiums: A=20
ratio below 100 indicates an underwriting profit, and one above=20
100 indicates a loss. When the investment income that an insurer=20
earns from holding policyholders' funds ("the float") is taken=20
into account, a combined ratio in the 107-111 range typically=20
produces an overall breakeven result, exclusive of earnings on=20
the funds provided by shareholders.

     For the reasons laid out in previous reports, we expect the=20
industry's incurred losses to grow by about 10% annually, even in=20
years when general inflation runs considerably lower. (Actually,=20
over  the last 25 years, incurred  losses have  grown at a still=20
faster rate, 11%.) If premium growth meanwhile materially lags=20
that 10% rate, underwriting losses will mount, though the=20
industry's tendency to underreserve when business turns bad may=20
obscure their size for a time.=20

     Last year we said the climb in the combined ratio was=20
"almost certain to continue - and probably will accelerate - for=20
at least two more years." This year we will not predict=20
acceleration, but otherwise must repeat last year's forecast.=20
Premium growth is running far below the 10% required annually.=20
Remember also that a 10% rate would only stabilize the combined=20
ratio, not bring it down.

     The increase in the combined ratio in 1989 was a little more=20
than we had expected because catastrophes (led by Hurricane Hugo)=20
were unusually severe. These abnormalities probably accounted for=20
about two points of the increase. If 1990 is more of a "normal"=20
year, the combined ratio should rise only minimally from the=20
catastrophe-swollen base of 1989. In 1991, though, the ratio is=20
apt to climb by a greater degree.

     Commentators frequently discuss the "underwriting cycle" and=20
speculate about its next turn. If that term is used to connote=20
rhythmic qualities, it is in our view a misnomer that leads to=20
faulty thinking about the industry's fundamental economics.

     The term was appropriate some decades ago when the industry=20
and regulators cooperated  to conduct the  business  in cartel =20
fashion. At that  time, the combined ratio fluctuated=20
rhythmically for two reasons, both related to lags. First, data=20
from the past were analyzed and then used to set new "corrected"=20
rates, which were subsequently put into effect by virtually all=20
insurers. Second, the fact that almost all policies were then=20
issued for a one-to three-year term - which meant that it took a=20
considerable time for mispriced policies to expire - delayed the=20
impact of new rates on revenues. These two lagged responses made=20
combined ratios behave much like alternating current. Meanwhile,=20
the absence of significant price competition guaranteed that=20
industry profits, averaged out over the cycle, would be=20
satisfactory.

     The cartel period is long gone. Now the industry has=20
hundreds of participants selling a commodity-like product at=20
independently-established prices. Such a configuration - whether=20
the product being sold is steel or insurance policies - is=20
certain to cause subnormal profitability in all circumstances but=20
one: a shortage of usable capacity. Just how often these periods=20
occur and how long they last determines the average profitability=20
of the industry in question.

     In most industries, capacity is described in physical terms.=20
In the insurance world, however, capacity is customarily=20
described in financial terms; that is, it's considered=20
appropriate for a company to write no more than X dollars of=20
business if it has Y dollars of net worth. In practice, however,=20
constraints of this sort have proven ineffective. Regulators,=20
insurance brokers, and customers are all slow to discipline=20
companies that strain their resources. They also acquiesce when=20
companies grossly overstate their true capital. Hence, a company=20
can write a great deal of business with very little capital if it=20
is so inclined. At bottom, therefore, the amount of industry=20
capacity at any particular moment primarily depends on the mental=20
state of insurance managers.=20

     All this understood, it is not very difficult to=20
prognosticate the industry's profits. Good profits will be=20
realized only when there is a shortage of capacity. Shortages=20
will occur only when insurers are frightened. That happens rarely=20
- and most assuredly is not happening now.

     Some analysts have argued that the more onerous taxes=20
recently imposed on the insurance industry and 1989's=20
catastrophes - Hurricane Hugo and the California earthquake -=20
will cause prices to strengthen significantly. We disagree. These=20
adversities have not destroyed the eagerness of insurers to write=20
business at present prices. Therefore, premium volume won't grow=20
by 10% in 1990, which means the negative underwriting trend will=20
not reverse.

     The industry will meantime say it needs higher prices to=20
achieve profitability matching that of the average American=20
business. Of course it does. So does the steel business. But=20
needs and desires have nothing to do with the long-term=20
profitability of industries. Instead, economic fundamentals=20
determine the outcome. Insurance profitability will improve only=20
when virtually all insurers are turning away business <i>despite</i>=20
higher prices. And we're a long way from that point.

     Berkshire's premium volume may drop to $150 million or so in=20
1990 (from a high of $1 billion in 1986), partly because our=20
traditional business continues to shrink and partly because the=20
contract under which we received 7% of the business of Fireman's=20
Fund expired last August. Whatever the size of the drop, it will=20
not disturb us. We have no interest in writing insurance that=20
carries a mathematical expectation of loss; we experience enough=20
disappointments doing transactions we believe to carry an=20
expectation of profit.

     However, our appetite for appropriately-priced business is=20
ample, as one tale from 1989 will tell. It concerns "CAT covers,"=20
which are reinsurance contracts that primary insurance companies=20
(and also reinsurers themselves) buy to protect themselves=20
against a single catastrophe, such as a tornado or hurricane,=20
that produces losses from a large number of policies. In these=20
contracts, the primary insurer might retain the loss from a=20
single event up to a maximum of, say, $10 million, buying various=20
layers of reinsurance above that level. When losses exceed the=20
retained amount, the reinsurer typically pays 95% of the excess=20
up to its contractual limit, with the primary insurer paying the=20
remainder. (By requiring the primary insurer to keep 5% of each=20
layer, the reinsurer leaves him with a financial stake in each=20
loss settlement and guards against his throwing away the=20
reinsurer's money.)

     CAT covers are usually one-year policies that also provide=20
for one automatic reinstatement, which requires a primary insurer=20
whose coverage has been exhausted by a catastrophe to buy a=20
second cover for the balance of the year in question by paying=20
another premium. This provision protects the primary company from=20
being "bare" for even a brief period after a first catastrophic=20
event. The duration of "an event" is usually limited by contract=20
to any span of 72 hours designated by the primary company. Under=20
this definition, a wide-spread storm, causing damage for three=20
days, will be classified as a single event if it arises from a=20
single climatic cause. If the storm lasts four days, however, the=20
primary company will file a claim carving out the 72 consecutive=20
hours during which it suffered the greatest damage. Losses that=20
occurred outside that period will be treated as arising from a=20
separate event.

     In 1989, two unusual things happened. First, Hurricane Hugo=20
generated $4 billion or more of insured loss, at a pace, however,=20
that caused the vast damage in the Carolinas to occur slightly=20
more than 72 hours after the equally severe damage in the=20
Caribbean. Second, the California earthquake hit within weeks,=20
causing insured damage that was difficult to estimate, even well=20
after the event. Slammed by these two - or possibly three - major=20
catastrophes, some primary insurers, and also many reinsurers=20
that had themselves bought CAT protection, either used up their=20
automatic second cover or became uncertain as to whether they had=20
done so.

     At that point sellers of CAT policies had lost a huge amount=20
of money - perhaps twice because of the reinstatements - and not=20
taken in much in premiums. Depending upon many  variables, a CAT=20
premium  might generally have run 3% to 15% of the amount of=20
protection purchased. For some years, we've thought premiums of=20
that kind inadequate and have stayed away from the business.

     But because the 1989 disasters left many insurers either=20
actually or possibly bare, and also left most CAT writers licking=20
their wounds, there was an immediate shortage after the=20
earthquake of much-needed catastrophe coverage. Prices instantly=20
became attractive, particularly for the reinsurance that CAT=20
writers themselves buy. Just as instantly, Berkshire Hathaway=20
offered to write up to $250 million of catastrophe coverage,=20
advertising that proposition in trade publications. Though we did=20
not write all the business we sought, we did in a busy ten days=20
book a substantial amount.

     Our willingness to put such a huge sum on the line for a=20
loss that could occur tomorrow sets us apart from any reinsurer=20
in the world. There are, of course, companies that sometimes=20
write $250 million or even far more of catastrophe coverage. But=20
they do so only when they can, in turn, reinsure a large=20
percentage of the business with other companies. When they can't=20
"lay off" in size, they disappear from the market.

     Berkshire's policy, conversely, is to retain the business we=20
write rather than lay it off. When rates carry an expectation of=20
profit, we want to assume as much risk as is prudent. And in our=20
case, that's a lot.

     We will accept more reinsurance risk for our own account=20
than any other company because of two factors: (1) by the=20
standards of regulatory accounting, we have a net worth in our=20
insurance companies of about $6 billion - the second highest=20
amount in the United States; and (2) we simply don't care what=20
earnings we report quarterly, or even annually, just as long as=20
the decisions leading to those earnings (or losses) were reached=20
intelligently.

     Obviously, if we write $250 million of catastrophe coverage=20
and retain it all ourselves, there is some probability that we=20
will lose the full $250 million in a single quarter. That=20
probability is low, but it is not zero. If we had a loss of that=20
magnitude, our after-tax cost would be about $165 million. Though=20
that is far more than Berkshire normally earns in a quarter, the=20
damage would be a blow only to our pride, not to our well-being.

     This posture is one few insurance managements will assume.=20
Typically, they are willing to write scads of business on terms=20
that almost guarantee them mediocre returns on equity. But they=20
do not want to expose themselves to an embarrassing single-
quarter loss, even if the managerial strategy that causes the=20
loss promises, over time, to produce superior results. I can=20
understand their thinking: What is best for their owners is not=20
necessarily best for the managers. Fortunately Charlie and I have=20
both total job security and financial interests that are=20
identical with those of our shareholders. We are willing to <i>look</i>=20
foolish as long as we don't feel we have <i>acted</i> foolishly.=20

     Our method of operation, incidentally, makes us a=20
stabilizing force in the industry. We add huge capacity when=20
capacity is short and we become less competitive only when=20
capacity is abundant. Of course, we don't follow this policy in=20
the interest of stabilization - we follow it because we believe=20
it to be the most sensible and profitable course of action.=20
Nevertheless, our behavior steadies the  market. In  this case,=20
Adam  Smith's  invisible  hand works as advertised.

     Currently, we hold an exceptional amount of float compared=20
to premium volume. This circumstance should produce quite=20
favorable insurance results for us during the next few years as=20
it did in 1989. Our underwriting losses should be tolerable and=20
our investment income from policyholder funds large. This=20
pleasant situation, however, will gradually deteriorate as our=20
float runs off.

     At some point, however, there will be an opportunity for us=20
to write large amounts of profitable business. Mike Goldberg and=20
his management team of Rod Eldred, Dinos Iordanou, Ajit Jain,=20
Phil Urban, and Don Wurster continue to position us well for this=20
eventuality.


