<b>Zero-Coupon Securities</b>

     In September, Berkshire issued $902.6 million principal=20
amount of Zero-Coupon Convertible Subordinated Debentures, which=20
are now listed on the New York Stock Exchange. Salomon Brothers=20
handled the underwriting in superb fashion, providing us helpful=20
advice and a flawless execution.=20

     Most bonds, of course, require regular payments of interest,=20
usually semi-annually. A zero-coupon bond, conversely, requires=20
no current interest payments; instead, the investor receives his=20
yield by purchasing the security at a significant discount from=20
maturity value. The effective interest rate is determined by the=20
original issue price, the maturity value, and the amount of time=20
between issuance and maturity.

     In our case, the bonds were issued at 44.314% of maturity=20
value and are due in 15 years. For investors purchasing the=20
bonds, that is the mathematical equivalent of a 5.5% current=20
payment compounded semi-annually. Because we received only=20
44.31 cents on the dollar, our proceeds from this offering were=20
$400 million (less about $9.5 million of offering expenses).

     The bonds were issued in denominations of $10,000 and each=20
bond is convertible into .4515 shares of Berkshire Hathaway.=20
Because a $10,000 bond cost $4,431, this means that the=20
conversion price was $9,815 per Berkshire share, a 15% premium to=20
the market price then existing. Berkshire can call the bonds at=20
any time after  September 28, 1992 at their accreted value (the=20
original issue price plus 5.5% compounded semi-annually) and on=20
two specified days, September 28 of 1994 and 1999, the=20
bondholders can require Berkshire to buy the securities at their=20
accreted value.

     For tax purposes, Berkshire is entitled to deduct the 5.5%=20
interest accrual each year, even though we make no payments to=20
the bondholders. Thus the net effect to us, resulting from the=20
reduced taxes, is positive cash flow. That is a very significant=20
benefit. Some unknowable variables prevent us from calculating=20
our exact effective rate of interest, but under all circumstances=20
it will be well below 5.5%. There is meanwhile a symmetry to the=20
tax law: Any taxable holder of the bonds must pay tax each year=20
on the 5.5% interest, even though he receives no cash.

     Neither our bonds nor those of certain other companies that=20
issued similar bonds last year (notably Loews and Motorola)=20
resemble the great bulk of zero-coupon bonds that have been=20
issued in recent years. Of these, Charlie and I have been, and=20
will continue to be, outspoken critics. As I will later explain,=20
such bonds have often been used in the most deceptive of ways and=20
with deadly consequences to investors. But before we tackle that=20
subject, let's travel back to Eden, to a time when the apple had=20
not yet been bitten.

     If you're my age you bought your first zero-coupon bonds=20
during World War II, by purchasing the famous Series E U. S.=20
Savings Bond, the most widely-sold bond issue in history. (After=20
the war, these bonds were held by one out of two U. S.=20
households.) Nobody, of course, called the Series E a zero-coupon=20
bond, a term in fact that I doubt had been invented. But that's=20
precisely what the Series E was.

     These bonds came in denominations as small as $18.75. That=20
amount purchased a $25 obligation of the United States government=20
due in 10 years, terms that gave the buyer a compounded annual=20
return of 2.9%. At the time, this was an attractive offer: the=20
2.9% rate was higher than that generally available on Government=20
bonds and the holder faced no market-fluctuation risk, since he=20
could at any time cash in his bonds with only a minor reduction=20
in interest.

     A second form of zero-coupon U. S. Treasury issue, also=20
benign and useful, surfaced in the last decade. One problem with=20
a normal bond is that even though it pays a given interest rate -=20
say 10% - the holder cannot be assured that a compounded 10%=20
return will be realized. For that rate to materialize, each semi-
annual coupon must be reinvested at 10% as it is received. If=20
current interest rates are, say, only 6% or 7% when these coupons=20
come due, the holder will be unable to compound his money over=20
the life of the bond at the advertised rate. For pension funds or=20
other investors with long-term liabilities, "reinvestment risk"=20
of this type can be a serious problem. Savings Bonds might have=20
solved it, except that they are issued only to individuals and=20
are unavailable in large denominations. What big buyers needed=20
was huge quantities of "Savings Bond Equivalents."

     Enter some ingenious and, in this case, highly useful=20
investment bankers (led, I'm happy to say, by Salomon Brothers).=20
They created the instrument desired by "stripping" the semi-
annual coupons from standard Government issues. Each coupon, once=20
detached, takes on the essential character of a Savings Bond=20
since it represents a single sum due sometime in the future. For=20
example, if you strip the 40 semi-annual coupons from a U. S.=20
Government Bond due in the year 2010, you will have 40 zero-
coupon bonds, with maturities from six months to 20 years, each=20
of which can then be bundled with other coupons of like maturity=20
and marketed. If current interest rates are, say, 10% for all=20
maturities, the six-month issue will sell for 95.24% of maturity=20
value and the 20-year issue will sell for 14.20%. The purchaser=20
of any given maturity is thus guaranteed a compounded rate of 10%=20
for his entire holding period. Stripping of government bonds has=20
occurred on a large scale in recent years, as long-term=20
investors, ranging from pension funds to individual IRA accounts,=20
recognized these high-grade, zero-coupon issues to be well suited=20
to their needs.

     But as happens in Wall Street all too often, what the wise=20
do in the beginning, fools do in the end. In the last few years=20
zero-coupon bonds (and their functional equivalent, pay-in-kind=20
bonds, which distribute additional PIK bonds semi-annually as=20
interest instead of paying cash) have been issued in enormous=20
quantities by ever-junkier credits. To these issuers, zero (or=20
PIK) bonds offer one overwhelming advantage:  It is impossible to=20
default on a promise to pay nothing. Indeed, if LDC governments=20
had issued no debt in the 1970's other than long-term zero-coupon=20
obligations, they would now have a spotless record as debtors.

     This principle at work - that you need not default for a=20
long time if you solemnly promise to pay nothing for a long time=20
- has not been lost on promoters and investment bankers seeking=20
to finance ever-shakier deals. But its acceptance by lenders took=20
a while: When the leveraged buy-out craze began some years back,=20
purchasers could borrow only on a reasonably sound basis, in=20
which conservatively-estimated <i>free</i> cash flow - that is,=20
operating earnings plus depreciation and amortization less=20
normalized capital expenditures - was adequate to cover both=20
interest and modest reductions in debt.

     Later, as the adrenalin of deal-makers surged, businesses=20
began to be purchased at prices so high that all free cash flow=20
necessarily had to be allocated to the payment of interest. That=20
left nothing for the paydown of debt. In effect, a Scarlett=20
O'Hara "I'll think about it tomorrow" position in respect to=20
principal payments was taken by borrowers and accepted by a new=20
breed of lender, the buyer of original-issue junk bonds. Debt now=20
became something to be refinanced rather than repaid. The change=20
brings to mind a <i>New Yorker</i> cartoon in which the grateful=20
borrower rises to shake the hand of the bank's lending officer=20
and gushes: "I don't know how I'll ever repay you."

     Soon borrowers found even the new, lax standards intolerably=20
binding. To induce lenders to finance even sillier transactions,=20
they introduced an abomination, EBDIT - Earnings Before=20
Depreciation, Interest and Taxes - as the test of a company's=20
ability to pay interest. Using this sawed-off yardstick, the=20
borrower ignored depreciation as an expense on the theory that it=20
did not require a current cash outlay.=20

     Such an attitude is clearly delusional. At 95% of American=20
businesses, capital expenditures that over time roughly=20
approximate depreciation are a necessity and are every bit as=20
real an expense as labor or utility costs. Even a high school=20
dropout knows that to finance a car he must have income that=20
covers not only interest and operating expenses, but also=20
realistically-calculated depreciation. He would be laughed out of=20
the bank if he started talking about EBDIT.=20

     Capital outlays at a business can be skipped, of course, in=20
any given month, just as a human can skip a day or even a week of=20
eating. But if the skipping becomes routine and is not made up,=20
the body weakens and eventually dies. Furthermore, a start-and-
stop feeding policy will over time produce a less healthy=20
organism, human or corporate, than that produced by a steady=20
diet. As businessmen, Charlie and I relish having competitors who=20
are unable to fund capital expenditures.

     You might think that waving away a major expense such as=20
depreciation in an attempt to make a terrible deal look like a=20
good one hits the limits of Wall Street's ingenuity. If so, you=20
haven't been paying attention during the past few years.=20
Promoters needed to find a way to justify even pricier=20
acquisitions. Otherwise, they risked - heaven forbid! - losing=20
deals to other promoters with more "imagination."

     So, stepping through the Looking Glass, promoters and their=20
investment bankers proclaimed that EBDIT should now be measured=20
against cash interest only, which meant that interest accruing on=20
zero-coupon or PIK bonds could be ignored when the financial=20
feasibility of a transaction was being assessed. This approach=20
not only relegated depreciation expense to the let's-ignore-it=20
corner, but gave similar treatment to what was usually a=20
significant portion of interest expense. To their shame, many=20
professional investment managers went along with this nonsense,=20
though they usually were careful to do so only with clients'=20
money, not their own. (Calling these managers "professionals" is=20
actually too kind; they should be designated "promotees.")

     Under this new standard, a business earning, say, $100=20
million pre-tax and having debt on which $90 million of interest=20
must be paid currently, might use a zero-coupon or PIK issue to=20
incur another $60 million of annual interest that would accrue=20
and compound but not come due for some years. The rate on these=20
issues would typically be very high, which means that the=20
situation in year 2 might be $90 million cash interest plus $69=20
million accrued interest, and so on as the compounding proceeds.=20
Such high-rate reborrowing schemes, which a few years ago were=20
appropriately confined to the waterfront,  soon became models of=20
modern finance at virtually all major investment banking houses.

     When they make these offerings, investment bankers display=20
their humorous side: They dispense income and balance sheet=20
projections extending five or more years into the future for=20
companies they barely had heard of a few months earlier. If you=20
are shown such schedules, I suggest that you join in the fun: =20
Ask the investment banker for the <i>one-year</i> budgets that his own=20
firm prepared as the last few years began and then compare these=20
with what actually happened.

     Some time ago Ken Galbraith, in his witty and insightful=20
<i>The Great Crash</i>, coined a new economic term: "the bezzle,"=20
defined as the current amount of undiscovered embezzlement. This=20
financial creature has a magical quality: The embezzlers are richer=20
by the amount of the bezzle, while the embezzlees do not yet feel=20
poorer.

     Professor Galbraith astutely pointed out that this sum=20
should be added to the National Wealth so that we might know the=20
Psychic National Wealth. Logically, a society that wanted to <i>feel</i>=20
enormously prosperous would both encourage its citizens to=20
embezzle and try not to detect the crime. By this means, "wealth"=20
would balloon though not an erg of productive work had been done.=20

     The satirical nonsense of the bezzle is dwarfed by the real-
world nonsense of the zero-coupon bond. With zeros, one party to=20
a contract can experience "income" without his opposite=20
experiencing the pain of expenditure. In our illustration, a=20
company capable of earning only $100 million dollars annually -=20
and therefore capable of paying only that much in interest -=20
magically creates "earnings" for bondholders of $150 million. As=20
long as major investors willingly don their Peter Pan wings and=20
repeatedly say "I believe," there is no limit to how much=20
"income" can be created by the zero-coupon bond.

     Wall Street welcomed this invention with the enthusiasm=20
less-enlightened folk might reserve for the wheel or the plow.=20
Here, finally, was an instrument that would let the Street make=20
deals at prices no longer limited by actual earning power. The=20
result, obviously, would be more transactions: Silly prices will=20
always attract sellers. And, as Jesse Unruh might have put it,=20
transactions are the mother's milk of finance.

     The zero-coupon or PIK bond possesses one additional=20
attraction for the promoter and investment banker, which is that=20
the time elapsing between folly and failure can be stretched out.=20
This is no small benefit. If the period before all costs must be=20
faced is long, promoters can create a string of foolish deals -=20
and take in lots of fees - before any chickens come home to roost=20
from their earlier ventures.=20

     But in the end, alchemy, whether it is metallurgical or=20
financial, fails. A base business can not be transformed into a=20
golden business by tricks of accounting or capital structure. The=20
man claiming to be a financial alchemist may become rich. But=20
gullible investors rather than business achievements will usually=20
be the source of his wealth.

     Whatever their weaknesses, we should add, many zero-coupon=20
and PIK bonds will not default. We have in fact owned some and=20
may buy more if their market becomes sufficiently distressed.=20
(We've not, however, even considered buying a new issue from a=20
weak credit.) No financial instrument is evil per se; it's just=20
that some variations have far more potential for mischief than=20
others.

     The blue ribbon for mischief-making should go to the zero-
coupon issuer unable to make its interest payments on a current=20
basis. Our advice: Whenever an investment banker starts talking=20
about EBDIT - or whenever someone creates a capital structure=20
that does not allow all interest, both payable and accrued, to be=20
comfortably met out of current cash flow <i>net of ample capital=20
expenditures</i> - zip up your wallet. Turn the tables by suggesting=20
that the promoter and his high-priced entourage accept zero-
coupon fees, deferring their take until the zero-coupon bonds=20
have been paid in full. See then how much enthusiasm for the deal=20
endures.

     Our comments about investment bankers may seem harsh. But=20
Charlie and I - in our hopelessly old-fashioned way - believe=20
that they should perform a gatekeeping role, guarding investors=20
against the promoter's propensity to indulge in excess.=20
Promoters, after all, have throughout time exercised the same=20
judgment and restraint in accepting money that alcoholics have=20
exercised in accepting liquor. At a minimum, therefore, the=20
banker's conduct should rise to that of a responsible bartender=20
who, when necessary, refuses the profit from the next drink to=20
avoid sending a drunk out on the highway. In recent years,=20
unfortunately, many leading investment firms have found bartender=20
morality to be an intolerably restrictive standard. Lately, those=20
who have traveled the high road in Wall Street have not=20
encountered heavy traffic.

     One distressing footnote: The cost of the zero-coupon folly=20
will not be borne solely by the direct participants. Certain=20
savings and loan associations were heavy buyers of such bonds,=20
using cash that came from FSLIC-insured deposits. Straining to=20
show splendid earnings, these buyers recorded - but did not=20
receive - ultra-high interest income on these issues. Many of=20
these  associations are now in  major trouble. Had their loans to=20
shaky credits worked, the owners of the associations would have=20
pocketed the profits. In the many cases in which the loans will=20
fail, the taxpayer will pick up the bill. To paraphrase Jackie=20
Mason, at these associations it was the managers who should have=20
been wearing the ski masks.


<b>Mistakes of the First Twenty-five Years (A Condensed Version)</b>

     To quote Robert Benchley, "Having a dog teaches a boy=20
fidelity, perseverance, and to turn around three times before=20
lying down." Such are the shortcomings of experience.=20
Nevertheless, it's a good idea to review past mistakes before=20
committing new ones. So let's take a quick look at the last 25=20
years.

<b>o</b>     My first mistake, of course, was in buying control of=20
Berkshire. Though I knew its business - textile manufacturing -=20
to be unpromising, I was enticed to buy because the price looked=20
cheap. Stock purchases of that kind had proved reasonably=20
rewarding in my early years, though by the time Berkshire came=20
along in 1965 I was becoming aware that the strategy was not=20
ideal.

     If you buy a stock at a sufficiently low price, there will=20
usually be some hiccup in the fortunes of the business that gives=20
you a chance to unload at a decent profit, even though the long-
term performance of the business may be terrible. I call this the=20
"cigar butt" approach to investing. A cigar butt found on the=20
street that has only one puff left in it may not offer much of a=20
smoke, but the "bargain purchase" will make that puff all profit.

     Unless you are a liquidator, that kind of approach to buying=20
businesses is foolish. First, the original "bargain" price=20
probably will not turn out to be such a steal after all. In a=20
difficult business, no sooner is one problem solved than another=20
surfaces -  never is there just one cockroach in the kitchen.=20
Second, any initial advantage you secure will be quickly eroded=20
by the low return that the business earns. For example, if you=20
buy a business for $8 million that can be sold or liquidated for=20
$10 million and promptly take either course, you can realize a=20
high return. But the investment will disappoint if the business=20
is sold for $10 million in ten years and in the interim has=20
annually earned and distributed only a few percent on cost. Time=20
is the friend of the wonderful business, the enemy of the=20
mediocre.

     You might think this principle is obvious, but I had to=20
learn it the hard way - in fact, I had to learn it several times=20
over. Shortly after purchasing Berkshire, I acquired a Baltimore=20
department store, Hochschild Kohn, buying through a company=20
called Diversified Retailing that later merged with Berkshire. I=20
bought at a substantial discount from book value, the people were=20
first-class, and the deal included some extras - unrecorded real=20
estate values and a significant LIFO inventory cushion. How could=20
I miss? So-o-o - three years later I was lucky to sell the=20
business for about what I had paid. After ending our corporate=20
marriage to Hochschild Kohn, I had memories like those of the=20
husband in the country song, "My Wife Ran Away With My Best=20
Friend and I Still Miss Him a Lot."

     I could give you other personal examples of "bargain-
purchase" folly but I'm sure you get the picture:  It's far=20
better to buy a wonderful company at a fair price than a fair=20
company at a wonderful price. Charlie understood this early; I=20
was a slow learner. But now, when buying companies or common=20
stocks, we look for first-class businesses accompanied by first-
class managements.

<b>o</b>     That leads right into a related lesson: Good jockeys will=20
do well on good horses, but not on broken-down nags. Both=20
Berkshire's textile business and Hochschild, Kohn had able and=20
honest people running them. The same managers employed in a=20
business with good economic characteristics would have achieved=20
fine records. But they were never going to make any progress=20
while running in quicksand.=20

     I've said many times that when a management with a=20
reputation for brilliance tackles a business with a reputation=20
for bad economics, it is the reputation of the business that=20
remains intact. I just wish I hadn't been so energetic in=20
creating examples. My behavior has matched that admitted by  Mae=20
West: "I was Snow White, but I drifted."

<b>o</b>     A further related lesson: Easy does it. After 25 years of=20
buying and supervising a great variety of businesses, Charlie and=20
I have <i>not</i> learned how to solve difficult business problems. What=20
we have learned is to avoid them. To the extent we have been=20
successful, it is because we concentrated on identifying one-foot=20
hurdles that we could step over rather than because we acquired=20
any ability to clear seven-footers.

     The finding may seem unfair, but in both business and=20
investments it is usually far more profitable to simply stick=20
with the easy and obvious than it is to resolve the difficult. On=20
occasion, tough problems <i>must</i> be tackled as was the case when we=20
started our Sunday paper in Buffalo. In other instances, a great=20
investment opportunity occurs when a marvelous business=20
encounters a one-time huge, but solvable, problem as was the case=20
many years back at both American Express and GEICO. Overall,=20
however, we've done better by avoiding dragons than by slaying=20
them.=20

<b>o</b>     My most surprising discovery: the overwhelming importance in=
=20
business of an unseen force that we might call "the institutional=20
imperative." In business school, I was given no hint of the=20
imperative's existence and I did not intuitively understand it=20
when I entered the business world. I thought then that decent,=20
intelligent, and experienced managers would automatically make=20
rational business decisions. But I learned over time that isn't=20
so. Instead, rationality frequently wilts when the institutional=20
imperative comes into play.

     For example: (1) As if governed by Newton's First Law of=20
Motion, an institution will resist any change in its current=20
direction; (2) Just as work expands to fill available time,=20
corporate projects or acquisitions will materialize to soak up=20
available funds; (3) Any business craving of the leader, however=20
foolish, will be quickly supported by detailed rate-of-return and=20
strategic studies prepared by his troops; and (4) The behavior of=20
peer companies, whether they are expanding, acquiring, setting=20
executive compensation or whatever, will be mindlessly imitated.

     Institutional dynamics, not venality or stupidity, set=20
businesses on these courses, which are too often misguided. After=20
making some expensive mistakes because I ignored the power of the=20
imperative, I have tried to organize and manage Berkshire in ways=20
that minimize its influence. Furthermore, Charlie and I have=20
attempted to concentrate our investments in companies that appear=20
alert to the problem.

<b>o</b>     After some other mistakes, I learned to go into business=20
only with people whom I like, trust, and admire. As I noted=20
before, this policy of itself will not ensure success: A second-
class textile or department-store company won't prosper simply=20
because its managers are men that you would be pleased to see=20
your daughter marry. However, an owner - or investor - can=20
accomplish wonders if he manages to associate himself with such=20
people in businesses that possess decent economic=20
characteristics. Conversely, we do not wish to join with managers=20
who lack admirable qualities, no matter how attractive the=20
prospects of their business. We've never succeeded in making a=20
good deal with a bad person.

<b>o</b>     Some of my worst mistakes were not publicly visible. These=20
were stock and business purchases whose virtues I understood and=20
yet didn't make. It's no sin to miss a great opportunity outside=20
one's area of competence. But I have passed on a couple of really=20
big purchases that were served up to me on a platter and that I=20
was fully capable of understanding. For Berkshire's shareholders,=20
myself included, the cost of this thumb-sucking has been huge.

<b>o</b>     Our consistently-conservative financial policies may appear=20
to have been a mistake, but in my view were not. In retrospect,=20
it is clear that significantly higher, though still conventional,=20
leverage ratios at Berkshire would have produced considerably=20
better returns on equity than the 23.8% we have actually=20
averaged. Even in 1965, perhaps we could have judged there to be=20
a 99% probability that higher leverage would lead to nothing but=20
good. Correspondingly, we might have seen only a 1% chance that=20
some shock factor, external or internal, would cause a=20
conventional debt ratio to produce a result falling somewhere=20
between temporary anguish and default.

     We wouldn't have liked those 99:1 odds - and never will. A=20
small chance of distress or disgrace cannot, in our view, be=20
offset by a large chance of extra returns. If your actions are=20
sensible, you are certain to get good results; in most such=20
cases, leverage just moves things along faster. Charlie and I=20
have never been in a big hurry: We enjoy the process far more=20
than the proceeds - though we have learned to live with those=20
also.

              *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *

     We hope in another 25 years to report on the mistakes of the=20
first 50. If we are around in 2015 to do that, you can count on=20
this section occupying many more pages than it does here.


<b>Miscellaneous</b>

     We hope to buy more businesses that are similar to the ones=20
we have, and we can use some help. If you have a business that=20
fits the following criteria, call me or, preferably, write.

     Here's what we're looking for:

     (1)  Large purchases (at least $10 million of after-tax=20
          earnings),

     (2)  demonstrated consistent earning power (future=20
          projections are of little interest to us, nor are=20
          "turnaround" situations),

     (3)  businesses earning good returns on equity while=20
          employing little or no debt,

     (4)  management in place (we can't supply it),

     (5)  simple businesses (if there's lots of technology, we=20
          won't understand it),

     (6)  an offering price  (we don't want to waste our time or=20
          that of the seller by talking,  even  preliminarily,=20
          about a transaction when price is unknown).

     We will not engage in unfriendly takeovers. We can promise=20
complete confidentiality and a very fast answer - customarily=20
within five minutes - as to whether we're interested. We prefer=20
to buy for cash, but will consider issuing stock when we receive=20
as much in intrinsic business value as we give.

     Our favorite form of purchase is one fitting the Blumkin-
Friedman-Heldman mold. In cases like these, the company's owner-
managers wish to generate significant amounts of cash, sometimes=20
for themselves, but often for their families or inactive=20
shareholders. At the same time, these managers wish to remain=20
significant owners who continue to run their companies just as=20
they have in the past. We think we offer a particularly good fit=20
for owners with such objectives. We invite potential sellers to=20
check us out by contacting people with whom we have done business=20
in the past.

     Charlie and I frequently get approached about acquisitions=20
that don't come close to meeting our tests:  We've found that if=20
you advertise an interest in buying collies, a lot of people will=20
call hoping to sell you their cocker spaniels. Our interest in=20
new ventures, turnarounds, or auction-like sales can best be=20
expressed by a Goldwynism: "Please include me out."

     Besides being interested in the purchase of businesses as=20
described above, we are also interested in the negotiated=20
purchase of large, but not controlling, blocks of stock=20
comparable to those we hold in Capital Cities, Salomon, Gillette,=20
USAir and Champion. Last year we said we had a special interest=20
in large purchases of convertible preferreds. We still have an=20
appetite of that kind, but it is limited since we now are close=20
to the maximum position we feel appropriate for this category of=20
investment.

