pleased in this instance to publicly compliment the Exchange.

              *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *

     Last summer we sold the corporate jet that we purchased for=20
$850,000 three years ago and bought another used jet for $6.7=20
million. Those of you who recall the mathematics of the=20
multiplying bacteria on page 5 will understandably panic: If our=20
net worth continues to increase at current rates, and the cost of=20
replacing planes also continues to rise at the now-established=20
rate of 100% compounded annually, it will not be long before=20
Berkshire's entire net worth is consumed by its jet.

     Charlie doesn't like it when I equate the jet with bacteria;=20
he feels it's degrading to the bacteria. His idea of traveling in=20
style is an air-conditioned bus, a luxury he steps up to only=20
when bargain fares are in effect. My own attitude toward the jet=20
can be summarized by the prayer attributed, apocryphally I'm=20
sure, to St. Augustine as he contemplated leaving a life of=20
secular pleasures to become a priest. Battling the conflict=20
between intellect and glands, he pled: "Help me, Oh Lord, to=20
become chaste - but not yet."

     Naming the plane has not been easy. I initially suggested=20
"The Charles T. Munger." Charlie countered with "The Aberration."=20
We finally settled on "The Indefensible." =20

              *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *

     About 96.9% of all eligible shares participated in=20
Berkshire's 1989 shareholder-designated contributions program.=20
Contributions made through the program were $5.9 million, and=20
2,550 charities were recipients.

     We urge new shareholders to read the description of our=20
shareholder-designated contributions program that appears on=20
pages 52-53. If you wish to participate in future programs, we=20
strongly urge that you immediately make sure your shares are=20
registered in the name of the actual owner, not in the nominee=20
name of a broker, bank or depository. Shares not so registered on=20
August 31, 1990 will be ineligible for the 1990 program.

              *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *

     The annual meeting this year will take place at 9:30 a.m. on=20
Monday, April 30, 1990. Attendance grew last year to about 1,000,=20
very close to the seating capacity of the Witherspoon Hall at=20
Joslyn Museum. So this year's meeting will be moved to the=20
Orpheum Theatre, which is in downtown Omaha, about one-quarter of=20
a mile from the Red Lion Hotel. The Radisson-Redick Tower, a much=20
smaller but nice hotel, is located across the street from the=20
Orpheum. Or you may wish to stay at the Marriott, which is in=20
west Omaha, about 100 yards from Borsheim's. We will have buses=20
at the Marriott that will leave at 8:30 and 8:45 for the meeting=20
and return after it ends.

     Charlie and I always enjoy the meeting, and we hope you can=20
make it. The quality of our shareholders is reflected in the=20
quality of the questions we get: We have never attended an annual=20
meeting anywhere that features such a consistently high level of=20
intelligent, owner-related questions.=20

     An attachment to our proxy material explains how you can=20
obtain the card you will need for admission to the meeting.=20
Because weekday parking can be tight around the Orpheum, we have=20
lined up a number of nearby lots for our shareholders to use. The=20
attachment also contains information about them.

     As usual, we will have buses to take you to Nebraska=20
Furniture Mart and Borsheim's after the meeting and to take you=20
to downtown hotels or to the airport later. I hope that you will=20
allow plenty of time to fully explore the attractions of both=20
stores. Those of you arriving early can visit the Furniture Mart=20
any day of the week; it is open from 10 a.m. to 5:30 p.m. on=20
Saturdays, and from noon to 5:30 p.m. on Sundays.

     Borsheim's normally is closed on Sunday, but we will open=20
for shareholders and their guests from noon to 6 p.m. on Sunday,=20
April 29th. Ike likes to put on a show, and you can rely on him=20
to produce something very special for our shareholders.

     In this letter we've had a lot to say about rates of=20
compounding. If you can bear having your own rate turn negative=20
for a day - not a pretty thought, I admit - visit Ike on the=20
29th.



